'''[[Thailand]]''' is a [[sovereign state]] located in the center of [[Mainland Southeast Asia]]. The country extends from the southeastern foothills of the [[Himalayas]] to the [[Mekong]] river, further south to the [[Gulf of Thailand]], and roughly to the middle of the [[Malay Peninsula]] and the [[Andaman Sea]] of the [[Indian Ocean]]. It has a total size of {{convert|513,120|km2|mi2|abbr=on}} which is the [[List of countries and dependencies by area|50th largest]] in the world.<ref name="cia1">{{Cite web |date=29 September 2025 |title=Thailand |url=https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/thailand/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251105175911/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/thailand/ |archive-date=5 November 2025 |access-date=5 Nov 2025 |website=The World Factbook |publisher=Central Intelligence Agency}}</ref><ref name=":5">{{Cite web |date=24 August 2023 |title=Where Is Thailand on the Map? |url=https://spectacularthailand.com/faq/where-is-thailand-on-the-map/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250519071127/https://spectacularthailand.com/faq/where-is-thailand-on-the-map/ |archive-date=19 May 2025 |access-date=5 Nov 2025 |website=Spectacular Thailand}}</ref> The land area lies between latitudes 5° 37' North and 20° 27' North, and between the longitude 97° 22' East and 105° 37' East.<ref name=":5" /> Around 70% of the Gulf of Thailand's coastline is Thai.<ref name=":52" /><ref name=":62" />

Thailand shares a {{convert|4,863|km|mi|abbr=on}} long international land boundary with [[Myanmar]], [[Laos]], [[Cambodia]] and [[Malaysia]]. The nation's axial position influenced many aspects of Thai society and culture.<ref name=":08">{{Cite book|url=https://www.loc.gov/item/88600485/|title=Thailand: a country study|date=1987|publisher=[[Federal Research Division]]|editor-last=LePoer|editor-first=Barbara Leitch|location=Washington, D.C.|pages=60–65|language=en|oclc=44366465|postscript=. {{PD-notice}}}}</ref> [[Southern Thailand]] is the only land route from mainland [[Asia]] to [[Malaysia]] and [[Singapore]]. It has an [[Exclusive economic zone of Thailand|exclusive economic zone]] of {{convert|305,778|km2|mi2|abbr=on}}.<ref name=":12" />

{{Short description|Science of classifying organisms}}
{{pp-semi-indef|small=yes}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=March 2022}}
{{Evolutionary biology}}
In [[biology]], '''taxonomy''' ({{ety|grc|''[[wikt:τάξις|τάξις]]'' ([[taxis]])|arrangement||''[[wikt:νόμος|-νομία]]'' ([[:wikt:-nomy|-nomia]])|[[Scientific method|method]]}}) is the [[science|scientific]] study of naming, defining ([[Circumscription (taxonomy)|circumscribing]]) and classifying groups of biological [[organism]]s based on shared characteristics. Modern approaches prioritize common ancestry and evolutionary relationships.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Classifying life |url=https://www.taxonomyaustralia.org.au/classifying-life |access-date=2026-04-20 |website=Taxonomy Australia |language=en}}</ref> Organisms are grouped into [[taxon|taxa]] (singular: taxon), and these groups are given a [[taxonomic rank]]; groups of a given rank can be aggregated to form a more inclusive group of higher rank, thus creating a taxonomic hierarchy. The principal ranks in modern use are [[domain (taxonomy)|domain]], [[kingdom (biology)|kingdom]], [[phylum]] (''division'' is sometimes used in botany in place of ''phylum''), [[class (biology)|class]], [[order (biology)|order]], [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], [[genus]], and [[species]]. The Swedish botanist [[Carl Linnaeus]] is regarded as the founder of the current system of taxonomy, having developed a ranked system known as [[Linnaean taxonomy]] for categorizing organisms.

With advances in the theory, data and analytical technology of biological systematics, the Linnaean system has transformed into a system of modern biological classification intended to reflect the [[evolution]]ary relationships among organisms, both living and extinct.

