The '''zodiac''' is a belt-shaped region of the sky that extends approximately 8° north and south [[celestial latitude]] of the [[ecliptic]] – the [[Sun path|apparent path]] of the [[Sun]] across the [[celestial sphere]] over the course of the year. Within this zodiac belt appear the [[Moon]] and the brightest [[planet]]s, along their orbital planes.<ref name="oxford-def">{{cite web|title=zodiac|url=https://en.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/zodiac|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160926223903/https://en.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/zodiac|url-status=dead|archive-date=26 September 2016|website=Oxford Dictionaries|publisher=Oxford University Press|access-date=19 September 2017}}</ref> The zodiac is divided along the ecliptic into 12 equal parts, called "[[astrological sign|signs]]", each occupying 30° of [[celestial longitude]]. These signs roughly correspond to the [[astronomical]] [[constellation]]s with the following modern names:<ref>Because the signs are each 30° in longitude but constellations have irregular shapes, and because of precession, they do not correspond exactly to the boundaries of the [[constellations]] after which they are named.</ref><ref>{{cite journal | last=Noble| first=William| title=Papers communicated to the Association. The Signs of the Zodiac. | journal=Journal of the British Astronomical Association | volume=12 | pages=242–244| bibcode=1902JBAA...12..242N| year=1902}}</ref> [[Aries (astrology)|Aries]], [[Taurus (astrology)|Taurus]], [[Gemini (astrology)|Gemini]], [[Cancer (astrology)|Cancer]], [[Leo (astrology)|Leo]], [[Virgo (astrology)|Virgo]], [[Libra (astrology)|Libra]], [[Scorpio (astrology)|Scorpio]], [[Sagittarius (astrology)|Sagittarius]], [[Capricorn (astrology)|Capricorn]], [[Aquarius (astrology)|Aquarius]], and [[Pisces (astrology)|Pisces]]. 

The signs have been used to determine the time of the year by identifying each sign with the days of the year the Sun is in the respective sign. In [[Western astrology]], and formerly [[astronomy]], the time of each sign is associated with different attributes. The zodiacal system and its angular measurement in 360 [[sexagesimal]] [[Degree (angle)|degree (°)]] originated with [[Babylonian astronomy]] during the [[1st millennium BC]], probably during the [[Achaemenid Empire]]. It was communicated into [[Greek astronomy]] by the 2nd century BC, as well as into developing the [[Hindu astrology|Hindu zodiac]]. Due to the [[precession of the equinoxes]], the time of year that the Sun is in a given constellation has changed since Babylonian times, and the point of [[March equinox]] has moved from [[Aries (constellation)|Aries]] into [[Pisces (constellation)|Pisces]]. 

The zodiac forms a [[celestial coordinate system]], or more specifically an [[ecliptic coordinate system]], which takes the ecliptic as the origin of latitude and the [[position of the Sun|Sun's position]] at [[Equinox (celestial coordinates)|vernal equinox]] as the origin of longitude.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=z3gRvA2J3DYC&pg=PA94 |title=A Compleat System of Astronomy |first=Charles |last=Leadbetter |year=1742 |publisher=J. Wilcox, London |page=94}}; numerous examples of this notation appear throughout the book.</ref> In modern astronomy, the ecliptic coordinate system is still used for tracking Solar System objects.

{{Short description|Scientific study of animals}}
{{Other uses}}
